First Author: Sangeetha
Title: HEL-MCNN: Hybrid extreme learning modified convolutional neural network for allocating suitable donors for patients with minimized waiting time
Source number: 16

Very short summary: This methods-focused paper proposed an MCNN-HELM model to allocate suitable donors while minimizing recipient waiting time. Five key findings were: (1) the model was evaluated on three real-time transplantation datasets spanning liver, heart, and lung transplantation; (2) the core model combined a modified convolutional neural network with a hybrid extreme learning machine and prairie dog optimization; (3) the approach explicitly aimed to prioritize high-risk patients while reducing waiting time; (4) the reported overall training accuracy was 97.5% with computational time 2.2 seconds; and (5) for the lung dataset, benefit-first allocation had lower death rate and higher average benefit/survival than real or FCFS policies in the reported simulation table. The article did not provide a conventional thoracic-transplant clinical cohort description or standard held-out diagnostic metrics such as AUC, sensitivity, or specificity.

